, "Jamie Ager's large fundraising edge ($3.1M raised, $2.2M on hand) with DCCC TV spending already live", "Prediction markets price Ager near 76%, well ahead of the district's R+5 lean", "Thin, dated polling: one Democratic-sponsored September poll showed Ager up 3"], "rationale": "The fundamentals favor Republicans in an R+5 district, but the open seat, Ager's dominant fundraising, active Democratic outside spending, and markets pricing him near 76% all point the other way. Raters mostly call it a tossup, so a modest Democratic edge of a couple points fits the evidence.", "watch": "Republican coalescing behind Jennifer Balkcom and a late red-meat nationalized campaign could close the gap quickly in a district that still leans right.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Chuck Edwards (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "NC-11 is an open seat this cycle after Republican Chuck Edwards withdrew from the ballot, turning a normally safe Republican mountain district into one of the cycle's most competitive House races. The district's partisan lean still favors Republicans by about five points, and most professional raters call the race a tossup, though Split Ticket goes as far as a Democratic flip.\n\nDemocrat Jamie Ager has built a substantial financial advantage, raising over $3 million with strong cash on hand, while the DCCC has already launched television ads. Prediction markets give Ager roughly a 76 percent chance of winning, and the only recent poll, from a Democratic sponsor, showed him narrowly ahead. The balance of evidence points to a small Democratic edge in a race that could easily swing back.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.5972, "margin": 1.39}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6249, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6236, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6239, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.5922, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.5614, "margin": 1.5}, {"d